{
  "mode": "server",
  "server": "cursor-cloud",
  "serverStatus": "ready",
  "serverDescription": "Cursor-internal cloud-agent diagnostics (server `cursor-cloud`). - `cursor-cloud-run-info` — return the current cloud-agent run identity, basic run metadata (name, repo, branch, model, owners — including the owning user's name and email when resolvable, lifecycle and setup status, created-at), and the URL where the run is available (https://cursor.com/agents/<bcId>). - `cursor-cloud-list-cloud-agents` — list cloud agents accessible to this run's principal in THIS run's environment (or, when this run has no saved environment, agents on the same repository), ordered by creation time (newest first), returning basic run metadata plus URLs. Users and service accounts see their own runs. Team admins can additionally browse teammates' runs only when enabled in team MCP settings. Supports browse filters (source, lifecycle status, setup status, recency/creation windows, archived, self-hosted, whether the agent made code changes, and whether it created a pull request) plus limit/offset paging. When counting or enumerating many runs, page with nextOffset until hasMore is false; use totalMatched only when the accessible scan is complete. - `cursor-cloud-environment-info` — return THIS run's environment (name, environment & version public ids, source, recorded-via/created-at provenance, repos, full environment.json, dashboard URL), effective egress policy (allow-all, the complete merged allowed-domain list, or unresolved with a reason), the environment build the pod booted from (build id, snapshot id, git-setup mode, warm-fork mode) or null when the run started just-in-time rather than from a build, and (for internal runs) the initiating Cursor client build info (version + commit SHA, same as Help → About). - `cursor-cloud-get-events` — list the events recorded for THIS run (the same events shown on the run's dashboard page), oldest first: run lifecycle and setup outcomes plus soft failures such as MCP authentication errors (kind `mcp_auth_error` — an MCP server rejected this run's credentials, so its tool calls failed while the run continued). Each event carries publicId, createdAtMs, category, logLevel, a stable kind, a user-facing title, and optional detail and linkUrl. - `cursor-cloud-batch-fetch-details` — fetch details for a batch of specified accessible cloud agents by bc_ids. Users and service accounts can fetch their own runs. Team admins can additionally fetch teammates' runs only when enabled in team MCP settings. When matching agents are found, writes basic run info to index.json and optionally writes diff-metadata.json, setup-logs.txt, start-logs.txt, environment-info.json, events.json, and transcript.json under /tmp/cursor/cloud-agent-transcripts/<datetime-id>/<bcId>/. Set include_events to include each run's dashboard events. First-class Task results include cloudAgentBcId; pass those ids to another batch-fetch-details call to fetch child transcripts. Transcripts can be very large — read them via subagents, never directly. - `cursor-cloud-get-automation` — look up one Cursor automation by id (UUID) via the AutomationsService GetAutomation API, as the run's principal: returns automation metadata (id, name, enabled state, display owner/creator, owner email, and URL) when readable, the same metadata fields for a teammate's private automation when only a restricted summary is visible, or a not-found error otherwise. - `cursor-cloud-list-environment-builds` — list this run's environment builds (the prebuilt environment snapshots a cloud agent can boot from), newest first, with status/failure outcomes. Supports statuses and created_after/created_before filters plus limit; page older builds by passing nextCursor back as cursor. - `cursor-cloud-environment-build-logs` — fetch the install/setup logs for one environment build by build_id, written to a file on this run's VM (the response returns the path; read the file, via a subagent if large). Build logs are retained ~10 days; only builds of this run's own environment are accessible. - `cursor-cloud-trigger-environment-build` — start one DRAFT environment build of this run's own environment and return its buildId. Only call this when the user explicitly asks to trigger a build; never call it on your own initiative. Supports per-repo ref overrides (`refs`) and a custom `environment_json` config override for testing config changes. For a greenfield run with no linked environment, pass the complete `environment_json`; the trigger creates and links a personal transitional draft automatically. Draft builds never become the build new agents boot from; track them with list-environment-builds and fetch logs with environment-build-logs. Presenting results: unless the user asks for a specific format or a summary, show a tool call's returned result to the user verbatim as pretty-printed JSON in a `json` code block rather than paraphrasing or summarizing it. This applies only to data returned directly by the tool call, not to detail files written to the filesystem (e.g. batch-fetch-details output).",
  "tools": [
    {
      "tool": "batch-fetch-details",
      "description": "Fetch details for a batch of specified accessible cloud agents by bc_ids. Users and service accounts can fetch their own runs. Team admins can additionally fetch teammates' runs only when enabled in team MCP settings. When matching agents are found, writes basic run info to index.json under a unique /tmp/cursor/cloud-agent-transcripts/<datetime-id>/ run directory, and can optionally write per-agent diff-metadata.json, setup-logs.txt, start-logs.txt, environment-info.json, events.json, and transcript.json files. Set include_events to include each run's dashboard events. First-class Task results include cloudAgentBcId, which can be passed to another batch-fetch-details call. Returns the run directory and written paths; transcripts can be very large, so read them via subagents, never directly.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "bc_ids": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
              "type": "string"
            },
            "minItems": 1,
            "maxItems": 50,
            "description": "Agent ids to fetch as a batch (max 50). Each id is access-checked and constrained to this run's environment (or, when this run has no saved environment, to agents on the same repository)."
          },
          "include_diff_metadata": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "Also write each agent's PR link/status and change counts to diff-metadata.json. Defaults to false."
          },
          "include_setup_logs": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "Also write each agent's pod setup and image-build logs to setup-logs.txt. Defaults to false."
          },
          "include_start_logs": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "Also write each agent's start-command stdout/stderr/exit lines to start-logs.txt (filtered from the same Redis setup stream as include_setup_logs; includes system starts like exec-daemon interleaved with the user start script). Defaults to false."
          },
          "include_environment": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "Also write each agent's environment identity/config and egress policy to environment-info.json. Defaults to false."
          },
          "include_events": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "Also write each agent's dashboard events to events.json. Defaults to false."
          },
          "include_transcripts": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "Also write each requested agent's full conversation to <bcId>/transcript.json. First-class Task tool results include cloudAgentBcId, which can be passed to a subsequent batch-fetch-details call. Defaults to false."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "bc_ids"
        ],
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "environment-build-logs",
      "description": "Fetch the install/setup logs for one environment build by build_id (bld-<YYYYMMDD>-<uuid>, as surfaced by environment-info's build block) and write them to a file on this run's VM under /tmp/cursor/environment-build-logs/, returning the path (logs can be large — read the file, via a subagent if needed). Logs cover the build's Docker image build plus clone/install/setup output and are retained for ~10 days after the build; older builds (or builds from before build-log capture shipped) return no logs and no file. Only builds of this run's own environment are accessible.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "build_id": {
            "type": "string",
            "minLength": 1,
            "description": "The environment build id to fetch install/setup logs for (e.g. bld-20260709-<uuid>)."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "build_id"
        ],
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "environment-info",
      "description": "Return the current cloud-agent run's environment (name, environment & version public ids, source, how/when it was recorded, repos, the full environment.json, and a link to the environment in the cursor.com dashboard), the effective egress policy (allow-all, the complete merged allowed-domain list, or unresolved with a reason), the environment build the pod booted from (build id, snapshot id, git-setup mode, warm-fork mode) or null when the run started just-in-time rather than from a build, and (for internal runs) the initiating Cursor client build info (version + commit SHA, same as Help → About). Use `environmentJsonPath` to tell repo-file managed environments from db-backed ones: a present path (defaulting to `.cursor/environment.json`) means the config is read from that repository environment.json (repo-file managed), while a null/absent path means a db-backed snapshot config (db-managed).",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {},
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "get-automation",
      "description": "Look up one Cursor automation by its id (UUID). Calls the AutomationsService GetAutomation API as the current run's principal and returns AutomationInfo metadata, enforcing the same read-access rules as the automations dashboard: automation id, name, enabled state, display owner/creator, owner email, and URL when readable; the same metadata fields for a teammate's private automation when only a restricted summary is visible; a not-found error otherwise (including when the automation exists but is not accessible).",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "id": {
            "type": "string",
            "format": "uuid",
            "description": "The automation's id, a UUID (e.g. 6e0d261c-86a2-4383-89f0-9162c1c10662). Find it in the automation's dashboard URL or in AutomationsService responses."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "id"
        ],
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "get-events",
      "description": "Return the list of events recorded for the current cloud-agent run — the same events shown on the run's cursor.com dashboard page — ordered oldest first. Events cover run lifecycle and setup outcomes (e.g. setup warnings, follow-up limits) and soft failures such as MCP authentication errors (kind `mcp_auth_error`: an MCP server rejected this run's credentials, so its tool calls failed while the run continued). Each event has publicId, createdAtMs, category (build | agent_setup | agent_run), logLevel (info | warn | error), a stable machine-readable kind, a user-facing title, and optional detail and linkUrl.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {},
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "list-cloud-agents",
      "description": "List cloud agents accessible to the current run's principal in this run's environment (or, for a run without a saved environment, agents on the same repository), ordered by creation time (newest first). Users and service accounts see their own runs. Team admins can additionally browse teammates' runs only when enabled in team MCP settings. Returns basic run metadata plus URLs. Filters: source, lifecycle status, setup status, recency/creation windows, archived, self-hosted, code changes, and pull-request creation. Paging: limit (default 50, max 200) + offset; the response includes returned, offset, limit, hasMore, nextOffset when more accessible agents were found, and totalMatched only when the accessible scan is complete. With many cloud agents, narrow with filters before paging through everything.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "sources": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
              "type": "string",
              "enum": [
                "desktop",
                "web",
                "mobile",
                "slack",
                "linear",
                "scm",
                "cli",
                "sand",
                "setup",
                "sdk",
                "automations",
                "api",
                "bugbot_autofix",
                "qabot_frontend",
                "local",
                "internal"
              ]
            },
            "maxItems": 50,
            "description": "Filter by agent source category, matching the source field returned in results. One or more of: desktop, web, mobile, slack, linear, scm, cli, sand, setup, sdk, automations, api, bugbot_autofix, qabot_frontend, local, internal."
          },
          "statuses": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
              "type": "string",
              "enum": [
                "UNSPECIFIED",
                "NOT_YET_STARTED",
                "RUNNING",
                "IDLE",
                "ERROR",
                "ARCHIVED",
                "EXPIRED",
                "WAITING_FOR_BACKGROUND_WORK"
              ]
            },
            "maxItems": 50,
            "description": "Filter by lifecycle status(es), matching the status field returned in results."
          },
          "setup_status": {
            "anyOf": [
              {
                "type": "string",
                "enum": [
                  "INSTALL_STARTED",
                  "INSTALL_SUCCEEDED",
                  "INSTALL_FAILED"
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "null"
              }
            ],
            "description": "Filter by user install setup status. INSTALL_FAILED indicates a confirmed failure, INSTALL_SUCCEEDED indicates a confirmed success, and INSTALL_STARTED indicates that setup started without a recorded outcome. null means no status was recorded and does not establish success or failure. Omit this field to apply no setup-status filter."
          },
          "include_archived": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "Include archived/killed agents. Defaults to false."
          },
          "active_since": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Only include agents with activity at or after this ISO-8601 UTC instant. Omitted by default."
          },
          "created_after": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Only include agents created at or after this ISO-8601 UTC instant."
          },
          "created_before": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Only include agents created at or before this ISO-8601 UTC instant."
          },
          "is_self_hosted_worker": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "Filter by where the agent runs: true for self-hosted private-worker agents, false for managed public-cloud agents, omit for both."
          },
          "did_make_code_changes": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "Filter by whether the agent made code changes (committed or not): true for agents with a recorded non-zero diff (files/lines changed), false for agents with no recorded changes, omit for both."
          },
          "did_create_pull_request": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "Filter by whether the agent created a pull request: true for agents with a PR, false for agents without one, omit for both."
          },
          "limit": {
            "type": "integer",
            "minimum": 1,
            "maximum": 200,
            "description": "Max number of agents to return (default 50, max 200)."
          },
          "offset": {
            "type": "integer",
            "minimum": 0,
            "description": "Number of matched accessible agents to skip before this page. Use the nextOffset from a previous response to page. Note: not a stable cursor — results are re-fetched and re-sorted each call, so offsets can shift if agents change between calls."
          }
        },
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "list-environment-builds",
      "description": "List this run's environment builds — the prebuilt environment snapshots a cloud agent can boot from instead of running install from scratch — newest first, so build ids can be found for environment-build-logs. Each entry carries buildId, status (IN_PROGRESS, SUCCEEDED, FAILED), failureType, source, triggerType, environmentVersionId, userFacingSnapshotId, createdAtMs, and completedAtMs. Filters: statuses, created_after, created_before. Paging: limit (default 25, max 100); when hasMore is true, pass the response's opaque nextCursor back as cursor to fetch the next (older) page. Builds exist only for environments that have environment builds enabled.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "statuses": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
              "type": "string",
              "enum": [
                "IN_PROGRESS",
                "SUCCEEDED",
                "FAILED",
                "CANCELLED"
              ]
            },
            "maxItems": 4,
            "description": "Filter by build outcome. One or more of: IN_PROGRESS, SUCCEEDED, FAILED, CANCELLED."
          },
          "created_after": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Only include builds created at or after this ISO-8601 UTC instant."
          },
          "created_before": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Only include builds created strictly before this ISO-8601 UTC instant."
          },
          "cursor": {
            "type": "string",
            "minLength": 1,
            "description": "Opaque pagination cursor: pass the previous response's nextCursor to fetch the next (older) page."
          },
          "limit": {
            "type": "integer",
            "minimum": 1,
            "maximum": 100,
            "description": "Max builds to return (default 25, max 100)."
          }
        },
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "run-info",
      "description": "Return the current cloud-agent run identity, basic run metadata (including lifecycle and setup status), and the URL where the run is available (https://cursor.com/agents/<bcId>).",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {},
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"
      }
    },
    {
      "tool": "trigger-environment-build",
      "description": "Start one DRAFT environment build of this run's own environment (fresh git checkout + install script, snapshotted at the end). Do NOT call this unless the user explicitly asked for a build to be triggered — each build boots a pod and runs the full install for up to a few hours. By default every repo in the environment builds at its default branch; pass `refs` (per repo_url) to build specific branches/tags/SHAs — every repo_url must belong to this run's environment. Note: for db-managed environments, a build created from a non-default ref can be tested but cannot be promoted to active later — only builds from every repo's default branch are promotable, so omit `refs` if you want a promotable build. Pass `environment_json` (full environment.json contents as a JSON string) to build with a custom config instead of the environment's saved db-backed config. For a greenfield run with no linked environment, `environment_json` is required and creates a personal transitional draft automatically. The config is recorded durably on the build and never changes active environment settings. `environment_json` is not supported for repo-file managed environments (those whose config is read from a repository environment.json — see `environmentJsonPath` in environment-info) and is rejected for them; test repo-file environments by building off a branch instead — create a branch, edit its environment.json, and build that branch via `refs`. `environment_json` also cannot change egress settings: omit egressMode/egressAllowlist (the environment's current egress is inherited) or match them exactly, otherwise the trigger is rejected — egress changes must be made by a human in the environment's settings. Draft builds never become the build new agents boot from and skip warm-pool seeding, so they are safe for experiments; they appear in list-environment-builds (source AGENT, trigger MANUAL) and their logs are fetchable with environment-build-logs. Returns the new buildId immediately; the build runs in the background. Limited to a few in-flight builds per environment.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "refs": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
              "type": "object",
              "properties": {
                "repo_url": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "minLength": 1,
                  "description": "Repository URL, matching one of the environment's repositories (as shown by environment-info)."
                },
                "ref": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "minLength": 1,
                  "description": "Branch, tag, or commit SHA to build this repo at."
                }
              },
              "required": [
                "repo_url",
                "ref"
              ],
              "additionalProperties": false
            },
            "description": "Per-repository ref overrides. Every repo_url must belong to this run's environment; repos left out build at their default branch."
          },
          "environment_json": {
            "type": "string",
            "minLength": 1,
            "maxLength": 200000,
            "description": "Full environment.json contents (a JSON string: snapshot, build.dockerfileContents, install, start, etc.) to build with instead of the environment's saved db-backed config — for testing config changes before committing or saving them. Required when this run has no linked environment: the trigger creates a personal transitional draft, links it to the run, and builds this config. Recorded durably on the build for later inspection; never changes active environment settings or history. NOT supported for repo-file managed environments (those whose config is read from a repository environment.json — see `environmentJsonPath` in environment-info); the trigger is rejected. Repo-file environments are instead tested by building off a branch: create a branch, edit its environment.json, and trigger a draft build with that branch ref (via `refs`). Egress settings (egressMode / egressAllowlist) cannot be changed here: omit them (the environment's current egress is inherited) or match it exactly, otherwise the trigger is rejected — egress changes must be made by a human in the environment's settings."
          }
        },
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"
      }
    }
  ]
}
